ption_full":"Mirion Technologies, Inc. provides radiation detection, measurement, analysis, and monitoring products and services in North America, Europe, and the Asia Pacific. It operates in two segments, Medical, and Nuclear & Safety. The Medical segment offers radiation oncology quality assurance and dosimetry solutions; patient safety solutions for diagnostic imaging and radiation therapy centers; radiation therapy quality assurance solutions for calibrating and verifying imaging and treatment accuracy; and radionuclide therapy products for nuclear medicine applications, such as product handling, medical imaging furniture, and rehabilitation products. This segment improves the quality and safety of cancer care delivery; and supports applications across medical diagnostics and practitioner safety. The Nuclear & Safety segment focuses on addressing critical radiation safety, measurement, and analysis applications; and provides personal radiation detection, identification equipment, and analysis tools. The company also offers include radiation measurement and monitoring solutions, reactor instrumentation and control detectors, imaging systems and cameras, and waste management systems; laboratory and scientific analysis systems comprising gamma/alpha spectroscopy, alpha/beta counting, specialty detectors, and spectroscopy software; radiation measurement and health physics instrumentation; and contamination and clearance monitors. It serves hospitals, clinics and urgent care facilities, dental and veterinary offices, radiation treatment facilities, OEMs for radiation therapy, laboratories, military organizations, government agencies, industrial companies, power and utility companies, reactor design firms, and NPPs. The company was formerly known as Global Monitoring Systems, Inc. and changed its name to Mirion Technologies, Inc. in January 2006. Mirion Technologies, Inc. was incorporated in 2005 and is headquartered in Atlanta, Georgia.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Thomas D. Logan | Founder, Medical Group President, CEO & Chairman | Age 64 | Compensation: $1.9M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 3K people. Revenue per employee of $289K ranks in the below the sector median among Industrials peers, with revenue growing 11%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $33K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $5K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 7\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (3/10).","financial_summary":["Mirion Technologies, Inc. is a mid-cap industrials business with a $4.5B market cap on $925M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +9% over the past three years, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 1% against a 11% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests, with gross margins sitting at 44%.","The business throws off $107M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 12 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 4.98\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.2\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and thin 1.2\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Net dilution runs at 8.4% annually, transferring value from owners to stock-based comp, and insiders hold 3.5% of the shares, and profit capture is only 0.5\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Very elevated short interest (21.6% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting against the business","sev":"red","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Goodwill represents 52% of total assets \u2014 a large impairment risk hanging over book value","sev":"red","cat":"balance_sheet"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Carries a 67% premium to sector median EV/EBITDA (confirmed by 1 other valuation measure)","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.6697129909365558},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Industrials sector lagging the market significantly, underperforming by 6% over 3 months","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Cyclicality and capital intensity \u2014 order books turn on a dime, and the heavy fixed cost base that creates the moat also magnifies the downturn.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Supply chain reshoring costs and labour shortages \u2014 onshoring mandates raise capex requirements while a shrinking skilled-labour pool inflates operating costs.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Tariff and trade-policy exposure \u2014 industrial exporters sit squarely in the crosshairs of protectionist policy, and retaliatory tariffs can close markets without warning.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Piotroski score 7/9 \u2014 financials improving across profitability, leverage and efficiency","Founder-led \u2014 owner alignment that often matters more than any incentive plan"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Liquidity Mapping Around (MIR) Price Events","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/38/Liquidity_Mapping_Around_MIR_Price_Events_111226124401_1794505441.html","date":"2026-11-12","timestamp":1794505441.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Mirion Technologies (MIR) Could Be 24% Undervalued After Q3 Earnings And Guidance Lift","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/tech/nyse-mir/mirion-technologies/news/mirion